# Summary
Introduced: the proof-fencing change correctly tries to fail closed for stale or mismatched active-primary observations, but the just-activated handoff exception is scoped only to the refresh call that completed peering rather than to the primary's first refresh/installation of the new epoch route. This can strand the selected primary if another acting-set node triggers activation.
The new validation requires active storage-node refresh routes to pass validate_pg_primary_active_observation unless the PG is in the local just_activated_pgs set. That set is constructed only from complete_ready_pg_peerings() during the same refresh_node_heartbeat call. In a multi-node acting set, a non-primary node can be the heartbeat that makes the PG ready. The authority then marks the PG Active, chooses the deterministic primary, bumps the epoch, and returns an Active route only to the non-primary caller under the just_activated exception. The actual primary is still running its old Peering-epoch config. When the primary later refreshes, its heartbeat has the old observed_epoch and cannot update its PG observation. Because just_activated_pgs is empty on this later request, runtime_map_for_storage_node_refresh calls validate_pg_primary_active_observation, which requires a current-epoch Active observation and matching proof. The primary cannot produce that observation until it receives and installs the new Active route map, but the new validation prevents it from receiving that map. This creates a liveness failure/denial of service for the PG until lease expiry or manual recovery, and a non-primary storage node could repeatedly trigger this timing to keep a PG unavailable.

# Resolution
Fixed in https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/ca3bc078.

The storage-node runtime-map refresh path now carries the refreshing node id into Active PG route construction. Active route export still validates the bound primary's current Active observation, but there is one narrow handoff exception: if the only validation failure is `PgPrimaryMissingActiveObservation`, the refreshing node must be the selected primary. This lets the selected primary receive the new Active route map after a non-primary heartbeat completes peering, while non-primary refreshes fail closed instead of receiving an Active route before the primary has installed and reported the current Active state.

The regression `storage_node_refresh_hands_active_route_to_primary_after_non_primary_completes_peering` covers the reported sequence: node 2 can trigger activation for PG 77, but node 2 receives `PgPrimaryMissingActiveObservation`; frontend export remains closed; node 1, the selected primary, receives the handoff map and then reports Active, after which normal serving/export succeeds.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Identify whether a non-primary storage-node refresh can be the call that completes PG peering and activates the PG.
- [x] Confirm the activation chooses a deterministic primary different from the refreshing node and bumps the cluster epoch.
- [x] Confirm just_activated_pgs is local to that single refresh, so the chosen primary's later refresh does not receive the bypass.
- [x] Confirm a primary that has not installed the Active route still sends the old Peering epoch/state and cannot update to an Active observation before receiving the route.
- [x] Confirm the later primary refresh is rejected by validate_pg_primary_active_observation, leaving no serving PG primary.
## Report
Validated the liveness bug with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC and an LLDB trace. The PoC creates two healthy storage nodes with PG 77 acting set [1,2]. Node 1 first reports Peering; node 2 then sends the refresh that completes peering. The authority activates the PG and chooses node 1 as primary, but only node 2's refresh receives the local just_activated_pgs bypass. When node 1 later refreshes using its still-installed Peering epoch/route, refresh_node_heartbeat fails while constructing the runtime map: PgPrimaryMissingActiveObservation { pg_id: 77, node_id: 1, cluster_epoch: ClusterEpoch(7) }, and serving_pg_primary remains None. Targeted test output: `primary refresh after non-primary activation failed with: PgPrimaryMissingActiveObservation { pg_id: 77, node_id: 1, cluster_epoch: ClusterEpoch(7) } ... ok`. Code evidence: complete_ready_pg_peerings marks PGs Active, stores active_primary/proof, and bumps epoch at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1420-1467; bump_epoch clears node pg_observations at lines 523-527; refresh_node_heartbeat scopes just_activated_pgs to one call at lines 1969-1985; active_pg_route_for_storage_node_refresh calls validate_pg_primary_active_observation when just_activated is false at lines 321-371; validate_pg_primary_active_observation rejects missing primary Active observation at lines 3797-3820; storage-node heartbeats are derived from installed cluster_epoch/pg_routes at crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:414-423, and stale observed_epoch heartbeats return early without installing/updating observations at control_plane.rs:1512-1520. LLDB confirmed the chain: breakpoint at control_plane.rs:1461 in complete_ready_pg_peerings was reached from node 2's refresh_node_heartbeat at line 1977; later breakpoint at control_plane.rs:3816 was reached from active_pg_route_for_storage_node_refresh line 371 via runtime_map_for_storage_node_refresh and refresh_node_heartbeat line 1985 during node 1's later refresh. Valgrind was attempted but is not installed in the container; this is a logic/liveness issue rather than memory corruption.

